<h1>ISFDB Help File</h1>

<a name="HELPWN"><hr>
<h2>Working Name</h2>
The ISFDB is built around the concept of an author <i>working name</i>.
Since it is desirable to place all of an author's work on a single
bibliographic page - including work done under a pseudonym - a consistant
working name is important. The working name is the name by which the 
author is mainly known.
<p>
The working name should be entered as it would appear on the book's
cover or table of contents. Spaces should be present between double
initials. Examples of working names:
<pre>
      Robert A. Heinlein
      J. G. Ballard
      George R. R. Martin
</pre>

<a name="HELPLN"><hr>
<h2>Legal Name</h2>
This entry should be filled with the author's legal name. A great
deal of latitude is given to this field - it can be used to denote
the author's current legal name, birth name, or maiden name. The
point is help collect relevant information concerning the author.
For instance, it is more informative to known that Simon Hawke's
<i>birth name</i> name was Nicholas Valentin Yermakov, even though
his legal name has been changed to Simon Hawke. Unusual situations
like Hawke's should be amplified via a notes entry.
<p>
The legal name should be entered in lastname, firstname format,
with initials expanded. Examples of legal names:
<pre>
      Heinlein, Robert Anson
      Ballard, James Graham
      Martin, George Raymond Richard
</pre>

<a name="HELPBP"><hr>
<h2>Birthplace</h2>
This entry can contain almost anything, but two things should be kept in mind:
<ol>
  <li>The field is also used to help denote changes in citizenship or
  major changes in residence. For instance <i>Russia;USA</i> would be
  used to indicate an author born in Russia who later moved to the
  USA. Detailed information should be kept in the biographic area.
  <li>The field width is limited to 32 characters, so keep the entry 
  brief.
  <li>The ISFDB is frequented by an international audience; don't
  enter <i>Fresno</i> as a birthplace. If you must enter a city, be
  sure to include the country. In the future, you may be required to
  include the author's birthplanet.
</ol>

<a name="HELPBD"><hr>
<h2>Birthyear</h2>
This field should contain the birth <b>year</b>. The year must be
a 4-digit numerical entry. Do not enter month or day of birth; that
sort of detailed information should be entered into the biographic notes
section. Only enter a birthdate if you actually know it.

<a name="HELPDD"><hr>
<h2>Deathdate</h2>
This field should contain the <b>year</b> of death, and nothing else. More
detailed information should be entered into the biographic notes section.
Naturally, the field should be left blank if the author is still with us;
please don't enter: <i>Not yet</i>.

<a name="HELPPS"><hr>
<h2>Pseudonyms</h2>
Pseudonyms should be entered as they would appear on the printed page;
multiple pseudonyms should be separated by a semicolon. Example:
<pre>
      Frederick Longbeard; Tol E. Rant; Shaw Vinist
</pre>

<a name="HELPEM"><hr>
<h2>Email Address</h2>
The email address should be entered as one would use it in a mailer.
Multiple email addresses should be separated with a single comma.
Example:
<pre>
      tbisson@aol.com,tbisson@interport.net
</pre>

<a name="HELPWP"><hr>
<h2>Web Page URL</h2>
The web page URL should be entered as one would use it in a browser.
Multiple URLs should be separated with a single comma.
Example:
<pre>
      http://stuff.com,http://other.stuff.com
</pre>

<a name="HELPBN"><hr>
<h2>Biographic Notes</h2>
This is a free-form text area. You can enter just about anything you
want here, although it may experience some editing prior to insertion 
into the ISFDB. One bit of advice: a biography which is longer than the
author's bibliography looks a little ... indulgent.
<p>
Although anything can be inserted into the text area, certain restrictions
apply to HTML directives. We'd like to be able to edit biographic notes,
but putting HTML directives in a forms textarea confuses every browser on
the planet. To get around this problem, some convenience directives are built
into the ISFDB; they are in the form %%Directive,Argument%%. These 
directives are:

<ol>
  <li> %%A,Working_Name%% - <b>A</b>uthor directive. This will create
  a hotlink to the named author's bibliography. Real life examples:
    <pre>
    %%A,August Derleth%%
    %%A,J. R. R. Tolkien%%
    </pre>

  <li> %%F,Exact_Title%% - <b>F</b>ilm link. This will create
  a link to the Internet Movie Database. Note that the <b>exact</b>
  title (as recognized by the IMDB) must be used. Real life examples:
    <pre>
    %%F,Star Wars (1977)%%
    %%F,20 Million Miles to Earth (1957)%%
    </pre>

  <li> %%M,Email_address%% - <b>M</b>ail directive. This will create
  a mailto directive to the given address. The address is also used 
  as the displayed link label. By convention, it should be the last
  entry in the textarea since it forces a newline break and prepends
  the data with an "Email:" label. Real life examples:
    <pre>
    %%M,mcq@en.com%%
    %%M,janeyolen@aol.com%%
    </pre>

  <li> %%H,label##URL%% - <b>H</b>tml link. This will create
  a link to an external html. The "label" portion of the directive
  will be the hotlink displayed onscreen; the URL is the full URL
  path. Real life examples:
    <pre>
    %%H,web page##http://www.sff.net/people/wpshunn%%
    %%H,web page##http://www.en.com/users/mcq%%
    </pre>
</ol>
An example which uses both the A and M directives comes from the
Will Shetterly entry:
    <pre>
    Husband, and sometime collaborator, to %%A,Emma Bull%%.
    %%M,shetterl@maroon.tc.umn.edu%%
    </pre>

<a name="HELPTI"><hr>
<h2>Title</h2>
Titles should appear as printed. Please do not reorder leading 
title articles; that is enter: <i>The Broken God</i>, not
<i>Broken God, The</i>. The title sorting routine ignores
leading title articles, so this is unneccesary.
<p>
Variant titles can be entered using the '^' operator. Any number
of variant titles can be entered, in the form: 
<i>title</i>^<i>variant title 1</i>^<i>variant title 2</i>...
A variant title should be followed by a parenthetical hint
which indicates why the title is variant. Examples:
<pre>
  The Frozen Year^Fallen Star (UK)
  Shadow Over Mars^The Nemesis from Terra (1961 US)
  The Soul of Melicent^Domnei (rev 1920)
  Darkness Weaves with Many Shadows^Darkness Weaves (exp 1978)
  Boten der Finsternis^Messengers of Darkness (trans: Christine Priest 1979)
</pre>

<a name="HELPAE"><hr>
<h2>Author/Editor</h2>
The author field should contain the working name of the author.
<ol>
  <li> The "^" operator is used to denote pseudonyms. In plain English, it
  means "as". So an entry for Richard Bachman will show up as Stephen King^Richard Bachman. 
  <li> The "+" operator is used to denote collaborations. In plain English, it
  means "with". So an entry for Larry Niven working with Jerry Pournelle will show
  up as Larry Niven+Jerry Pournelle. 
  <li> Complex pseudonym constructions can be made with the use of parenthesis.
  For instance, the collaborative pseudonym Lewis Padgett can be denoted as:
  (C. L. Moore+Henry Kuttner)^Lewis Padgett 
</ol>

<a name="HELPYR"><hr>
<h2>Year</h2>
Normally, the year entry consists of a 4-digit entry. There are exceptions:
<ol>
  <li> The year 0 denotes an unknown publication date.
  <li> The year 8888 can be used to denote infamously unpublished books.
  <li> The year 9999 denotes a forthcoming publication date. This
  entry can also take an optional MonYr extension to show the projected release
  date.  "9999Apr96" denotes a forthcoming book expected to be released in Apr
  96. Note that the date must be two digits, while the 3 character month code
  must be one of: Jan, Feb, Mar, Apr, May, Jun, Jul, Aug, Sep, Oct, Nov, Dec. 
</ol>
<b>NOTE:</b> Please do not update the forthcoming publication date once you
see a book appear in bookstores. <i>All</i> of the forthcoming book dates will
be updated at one time at the end of the month.

<a name="HELPPB"><hr>
<h2>Publisher</h2>
The publisher's name goes here. Well-known genre-specific publishing houses
can be entered in short format, such as: <i>Tor</i>, or <i>Ace</i>. Lesser-known,
or non-genre houses should be entered in the traditional long form: 
<i>New York: Harmony Books</i>, or <i>London: Sphere</i>.

<a name="HELPIS"><hr>
<h2>ISBN</h2>
There are no entry conventions for the ISBN field. It may either be in
the dashed form: <i>0-553-18048-7</i>, or the non-dashed form <i>0553180487</i>.

<a name="HELPPP"><hr>
<h2>Pages</h2>
If no introductory material exists in the publication, simply enter the 
number of pages: <i>376</i>. Introductory material can be denoted by using
roman numerals: <i>viii+227</i>.

<a name="HELPPR"><hr>
<h2>Price</h2>
Prices, unless otherwise noted, are in US dollars. The dollar sign should be
entered. Canadian publications which share the same ISBN as the USA publication
can be entered: <i>$5.50 ($6.99 Can)</i>. UK prices should be preceded with
an 'L': <i>L8.99</i>.

<a name="HELPCV"><hr>
<h2>Cover Artist</h2>
Since the artwork database is constructed from the cover artist field, it 
turns out to be extremely pedantic; only two forms of entry are accepted:
<ol>
  <li> The artist's working name only: <i>Stephen Youll</i>.
  <li> An entry of the form: working_name for "title" is also accepted. The
  database compiler recognizes this format and strips off the trailing 
  information. Example: <i>Jason Hurst for "The Coming of Vertumnus"</i>.
</ol>
Entries that are unhelpful: <i>Looks like Bob Eggleton</i>, or <i>Title page
says Whelan, but the cover is signed George</i>. Please put such data in the
notes section.

<a name="HELPSE"><hr>
<h2>Series</h2>
If the work is part of a series, enter the series name here. If the work
belongs to a series which already has entries in the ISFDB, use that series
name.

<a name="HELPSN"><hr>
<h2>Series Number</h2>
The series number is really intended to disclose the order of publication.
Many series are produced at a rapid rate, sometimes several in a single
year. This number helps to show the correct order. We won't be very
responsive to numeration wars in which one group insists that the series
should be read in this order, while another group insists that the series
should be read in that order.

<a name="HELPSS"><hr>
<h2>Super Series</h2>
Authors may write a series of series, placing the works in the same
universe. The super series entry can be used for this purpose. <b>Note</b>:
a super series must only contain series names - it cannot contain works. That
is, a work cannot belong to <i>just</i> a super series without belonging to a
series first.

<a name="HELPSL"><hr>
<h2>Story Length</h2>
This is the categorical length, not the word count. It can be set to:
<ol>
  <li> nv. Novella.
  <li> nt. Novelette.
  <li> ss. Short Story.
  <li> sf. Short Fiction. Used when the story length isn't known.
  <li> jvn. Used only for novels - indicates a juvenille.
  <li> nvz. Used only for novels - indicates a novelization.
</ol>

<a name="HELPFORMAT"><hr>
<h2>Publication Format</h2>
This determines the publication format of the book. Valid selections are:
<ol>
  <li> hc. Hard Cover.
  <li> pb. Paperback.
  <li> tp. Trade Paperback.
  <li> ph. Pamphlet.
</ol>

<a name="HELPGN"><hr>
<h2>Publication Notes/Synopsis</h2>
This is a free-form text area. You can enter just about anything you
want here, although it may experience some editing prior to insertion 
into the ISFDB. Notes should be used to illustrate information not
well-handled by the ISFDB: interior illustrations, publication title
clarifications, or just trivia. Synopsis data should tell just enough
about the story without giving it away; it should not contain marketing
blurbs such as "Charles de Lint calls this the greatest novel ever written".
<b>Don't use copyrighted material for the synopsis</b>; this includes both
synopsis found elsewhere and from the book's cover.
<p>
Although anything can be inserted into the text area, certain restrictions
apply to HTML directives. We'd like to be able to edit biographic notes,
but putting HTML directives in a forms textarea confuses every browser on
the planet. To get around this problem, some convenience directives are built
into the ISFDB; they are in the form %%Directive,Argument%%. These 
directives are:

<ol>
  <li> %%A,Working_Name%% - <b>A</b>uthor directive. This will create
  a hotlink to the named author's bibliography. Real life examples:
    <pre>
    %%A,August Derleth%%
    %%A,J. R. R. Tolkien%%
    </pre>

  <li> %%M,Email_address%% - <b>M</b>ail directive. This will create
  a mailto directive to the given address. The address is also used 
  as the displayed link label. By convention, it should be the last
  entry in the textarea since it forces a newline break and prepends
  the data with an "Email:" label. Real life examples:
    <pre>
    %%M,mcq@en.com%%
    %%M,janeyolen@aol.com%%
    </pre>

  <li> %%H,label##URL%% - <b>H</b>tml link. This will create
  a link to an external html. The "label" portion of the directive
  will be the hotlink displayed onscreen; the URL is the full URL
  path. Real life examples:
    <pre>
    %%H,web page##http://www.greyware.com/authors/wpshunn%%
    %%H,web page##http://www.en.com/users/mcq%%
    </pre>
</ol>

<a name="HELPST"><hr>
<h2>Story Type</h2>
This is the entry type. The types are similar to those used 
by magazines on the Table of Contents page. Some of the
types are used to indicate story length, while others indicate
a type of non-shortfiction:
<ol>
  <li> nv. Novella.
  <li> nt. Novelette.
  <li> ss. Short Story.
  <li> sf. Short Fiction. Used when the story length isn't known.
  <li> se. Serial.
  <li> p.  Poem.
  <li> es. Essay.
</ol>

<a name="HELPXX"><hr>
<h2>Email Address</h2>
The email address is used for a number of purposes:
<ol>
  <li> It let's us know who submitted magazine information, so that we can 
  create the appropriate attributions.
  <li> It provides an address such that we can ask questions back to the
  submitter.
  <li> In the future, registration of email addresses may be required in order
  to enter data.
</ol>
Email addresses will not be collected, collated, or summarized in any way. They will
not be made available to parties outside the ISFDB.

